#text(font: "Source Serif 4", size: 8pt)[A synthetic edition · Browser reading fixture]
#v(.3in)
The table was the first thing we carried into the room. Before the chairs, before the lamp, before we had decided what to call the space, there was somewhere to put a cup of tea and open a book.

In the morning, light fell across the boards and made a map of their small imperfections. There was a pale ring near the corner, a line where two pieces of wood met, and one smooth patch where a hand had rested for years. None of these things made it less useful. They made it particular.

I began to notice the objects we reached for without thinking: the heavy glass, the blue pencil, the bowl that fitted a small handful of plums. These were ordinary things, chosen slowly. We kept them because they belonged to the shape of our days.

A book can be like that. The writing might begin as a letter read on a bright screen, between other things. On paper it asks for a little space. You can leave it open, come back to a paragraph, or hand it to someone sitting across the table.

There is no great ceremony required. A chair, a little light, enough time to finish a page. That is a good beginning.
